Practical No.6
Aim: To understand phishing and email spoofing through a safe simulation and identify techniques for detecting and preventing such attacks.
Requirements
Kali Linux / Windows 
Web browser 
Email sample created for the practical 
No real credentials or personal information 
Part A: Phishing Awareness Simulation
Step 1: Create a simulated phishing email
Use a text editor and create a sample such as:
Subject: Urgent: Account Verification Required

Dear User,

Your account requires immediate verification.
Please verify your account within 24 hours to avoid suspension.

[VERIFY YOUR ACCOUNT]

Regards,
Security Team
Do not actually send this message to real users.
Step 2: Identify the warning signs
Ask students to examine the message and identify:
Urgency – "within 24 hours" 
Threat – "account suspension" 
Generic greeting – "Dear User" 
Unexpected request – asks the user to verify an account 
Suspicious link – destination should be checked before clicking 
Sender identity – sender address should be verified 
Part B: Demonstrate Email Spoofing Concept
What is email spoofing?
Email spoofing occurs when an email is made to appear as though it came from a trusted sender, even though it was actually sent by someone else.
For example:
Displayed sender:
IT Support <it-support@example.com>

Actual sender:
unknown/untrusted source
The important point is that the displayed From name alone cannot prove who sent the email.
Safe simulation
Create a screenshot/mock email header or write the following on paper:
From: IT Support <it-support@example.com>
To: Student
Subject: Password Expiration

Authentication results:
SPF: FAIL
DKIM: FAIL
DMARC: FAIL
Ask students:
"Should this email be trusted?"
Expected answer:
No. The authentication failures are strong warning signs.
Part C: Examine Email Headers
If you receive a legitimate test email in your own account, open it and select:
Show original / View source / View message details
Look for:
From:
Return-Path:
Received:
Authentication-Results:
SPF:
DKIM:
DMARC:
Meaning
Term
Purpose

SPF
Checks whether a server is authorized to send mail for a domain

DKIM
Uses a cryptographic signature to verify email integrity/source

DMARC
Helps domain owners specify how SPF/DKIM failures should be handled

Part D: How to Defend Against Phishing
Users should:
Check the complete sender address. 
Hover over links before clicking. 
Never share passwords or OTPs through email. 
Be suspicious of urgent requests. 
Access banking/email services through their official app or manually typed website. 
Enable MFA. 
Report suspicious emails. 
Organizations should:
Configure SPF, DKIM and DMARC. 
Use email security filters. 
Conduct phishing-awareness training. 
Monitor suspicious login activity. 
Establish an incident-reporting process. 
