2. Simple Practical Simulation
Create a fake phishing message for classroom analysis.
Example:
Subject: Urgent – Your Account Requires Verification
Dear Customer,
Your account will be temporarily suspended unless you verify your account immediately.
Verify Account
Regards,
Security Team
Ask students to identify the warning signs.
Red flags
Creates urgency or fear 
Requests immediate action 
Uses a generic greeting 
Contains a suspicious link 
Requests confidential information 
May use a fake sender address 
Contains spelling/grammar inconsistencies 
3. Demonstrate Link Inspection
Instead of clicking a suspicious link, hover over it and examine the actual URL.
For example:
Displayed:
https://bank-example.com/verify

Actual:
https://bank-example-login.example.net/verify
The domain should be carefully checked before opening the website.
4. Defensive Measures
For individuals
Never share OTP, PIN, CVV or passwords 
Verify unexpected requests through an official channel 
Don't click suspicious links 
Check the sender's email address 
Use MFA 
Use strong, unique passwords 
Keep software updated 
Report suspicious messages 
For organizations
Conduct security-awareness training 
Implement email filtering 
Use MFA 
Apply least-privilege access 
Establish incident-reporting procedures 
Conduct authorized phishing-awareness exercises 
Monitor suspicious login activity 
5. Simple Classroom Exercise
Give students these three messages and ask them to classify them:
Message A
"Your bank account will be blocked in 2 hours. Click here immediately to verify."
Message B
"Hi, this is IT Support. Please send me your password so I can fix your account."
Message C
"Your monthly bank statement is available. Log in through your bank's official mobile application."
Students should identify:
A → Phishing 
B → Pretexting / impersonation 
C → Potentially legitimate, but independently verify 
Result
Thus, common social engineering techniques were explored, their warning signs were identified, and appropriate preventive measures were studied.
For a college practical, this is safer and simpler than actually sending phishing emails or collecting credentials.

